WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.790
- What's you're about
to watch in this room

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:06.040
is a strange mortal combat
between a man and himself.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.880
- Good day residents of the internet

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.640
and welcome back to the show that today,

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.800
pits two foreboding
anthologies against each other.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.950
One is primarily in black and white,

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.650
the other in glorious Technicolor

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.680
and I wanna live in neither

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.540
because it's "Twilight
Zone" versus "Black Mirror."

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.530
The OG show from the
genius mind of Rod Serling

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.220
is going up against stiff competition

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.120
with the modern look at ourselves

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.660
and what we could or already have become.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.870
It's the ultimate deconstruction
of two dystopian delights

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.360
that stand the test of time,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.400
a match up that could only
happen in "The Twilight Zone".

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.760
- [Rod] You've just crossed
over into ""The Twilight Zone"."

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.490
- Here's how we'll determine a winner.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.690
Round one Tomato Meters
slash audience score.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.430
Round two iconic moments.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.780
Round three, cultural relevance,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.050
and then we'll toss a wild
card round into the mix

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.570
that helps settle the score.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.240
And since this is the internet
and it lives on forever,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.690
if any of those pig doctors

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.410
from the eye of the beholder
episode are watching,

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.040
this is what beauty used to look like.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.390
All humans aiming for this.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.370
Ask any of your swine brethren.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.980
This is what handsome is.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:13.813
Got it?

00:01:13.813 --> 00:01:14.646
It's settled.

00:01:14.646 --> 00:01:16.193
Okay, now we can start.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:17.900
- Two stars?

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:20.000
Two stars?

00:01:20.959 --> 00:01:22.200
- Wasn't a meaningful encounter.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.410
- [Rod] They picked the most
dangerous enemy they can find,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.530
and it's themselves.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.690
- Round one Tomato Meter
slash audience score.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.290
Back when ""The Twilight Zone"" began,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.290
rotten tomatoes were just old veggies

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.050
you'd bring to a comedy show.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.440
Critical gauges have
certainly evolved since 1959,

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.260
but so has ""The Twilight Zone."

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.380
After its initial run through 1964,

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.070
the show took a long breather.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.600
It was then revived as a movie in 1983

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.980
and then a new series run through

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.630
the later part of the 1980s.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.000
It went dormant again,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.770
then returned on UPN
for one season in 2002,

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.210
like Pennywise the clown.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.760
It would then depart only
to return years later

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.030
in its current iteration on CBS

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.530
with Jordan Peele stepping into

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.840
the Rod Serling curator role.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.680
- [Jordan] Today, however,
she will have no choice

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.830
but to revisit history again and again.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.620
- Peele has certainly turned the Zone

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.230
back into a fresh property

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.120
after the ill-fated movie and UPN run,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.850
both earned rotten scores
on the Tomato Meter,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.850
but the show has yet to return
to peak form in the 1960s.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.560
The original Rod Serling era has

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.560
an average Tomato Meter score of 82%

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.010
and weighing it down is
it's only rotten season

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.500
during that time, season four at 57%.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.320
All told "The Twilight Zone"
carries an average of 64%,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.920
not bad for a concept that
has spanned half a century

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.340
and is still going strong.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.647
But what happens when the
Zone glances into the mirror?

00:02:46.647 --> 00:02:49.670
"Black Mirror" debuted overseas in 2011

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:53.420
and now has five seasons worth
of technological tomfoolery

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.770
brought to us by showrunners

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.567
Charlie Brooker and Annabel Jones.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:59.030
"Black Mirror" has an
impressive overall average

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.380
of 83% on the Tomato Meter.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.250
That's even more striking
when you consider

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.900
that the most recent
season is by far the lowest

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.630
with a still fresh 67%.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.320
Season one's set the high mark at 98%,

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.210
and it's last three seasons
on Netflix average 79%.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.260
For funsies that revolutionary
Bandersnatch project?

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.720
That was at 72%.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.470
- Sounds awesome!

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.310
I see a trend.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.340
Critics love these shows when they debut.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.100
They're new, they're
different, they're creative,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.380
and then it gets tougher and tougher

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.020
to match the original gut
punch as the shows carry on.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.520
Is that how audiences feel too?

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.700
The average audience score
for "Black Mirror" is 81%,

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.670
and that once again,

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.580
handily defeats "The Twilight Zone's"

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.400
barely fresh average of 63%.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.610
And oddly enough, it's
the Jordan Peele iteration

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.870
of "Twilight Zone" that's
weighing it down the most

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.940
at a rotten 39%.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.450
Balancing that out is the
original Rod Serling run

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.590
which tops anything
else in "Twilight Zone"

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.690
or "Black Mirror" at 96%.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.770
You see kids since the 1960s,
art has been all downhill.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.320
We had The Zone, we had The Beatles,

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.860
who were heavily
influenced by The Monkees.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.370
That should actually be an
episode of "Twilight Zone."

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.340
- You are obsolete, Mr. Wordsworth.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.640
- Speaking of "Black Mirror",

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.320
that show also is experiencing
its audience score low point

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.980
with its most recent season.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:21.240
Season five is being
a party pooper at 45%.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.650
Maybe the quality has dipped

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.018
or maybe 2020 feels like one long episode

00:04:26.018 --> 00:04:27.430
and we don't have the appetite.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.700
These shows are many things,

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.830
but uplifting ain't
necessarily one of them.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.550
May I suggest "Golden Girls"
or "Mama's Family" instead.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.680
- Better late than.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.513
(bell dinging)

00:04:38.513 --> 00:04:39.346
Blanche!

00:04:39.346 --> 00:04:40.317
- Pregnant!

00:04:40.317 --> 00:04:42.680
- "The Twilight Zone"
gets major points in this

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.920
and every round for inspiring
tons of artistic endeavors,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.380
including "Black Mirror",
but in this round,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.010
it's not just about how you start,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.650
it's how you finish and middle.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.210
Because of that "Black Mirror"
squeaks by with the win

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.430
and takes a surprising

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.135
one nothing lead over "Twilight Zone."

00:05:00.135 --> 00:05:01.280
(laughing)

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.113
- Jesus.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.600
- Round two iconic moments.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.510
And now is where "Black
Mirror" could shock the world.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.030
In just five short seasons,
the show has managed

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.510
to carve out its own unique
spot in the cultural zeitgeist,

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:18.190
a word I'm 70% I just utilized accurately.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:20.190
It's impressive enough
that "Black Mirror" managed

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.870
to separate itself from being labeled

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.320
just another "Twilight Zone" imitation.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.100
All the iconic moments
it's already provided us,

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.100
that's just icing on the cake.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.980
A cake that probably tastes
like licorice I'm betting.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.560
The most obvious example would be

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.250
the moment that surprised the world

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:38.310
and dominated social
media in "Bandersnatch,"

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.570
an interactive movie revolution
that allowed the viewer

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.070
to make decisions and suffer consequences.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.690
It was almost like a choose
your own adventure novel,

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:49.400
maybe a little too close.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.010
It caused a lawsuit by Chooseco,
who owns those book rights,

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.270
and I dare anyone to prove

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:57.750
that they had heard of
Chooseco prior to right now?

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.550
But that episode also
snagged two primetime Emmys

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:03.050
and for once, gave
viewers a positive outlook

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.070
on a future where we get to interact

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.760
with our favorite TV
shows in a whole new way.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.110
- It was ahead of its time
in as much as time exists.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:13.720
- That's a considerable left turn

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.350
from the norm that is "Black Mirror."

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:17.690
Usually tech either falls into our hands

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.310
or is already in existence

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.200
and it's that obsession
that leads to our downfall.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.760
Take the very first episode,
"The National Anthem."

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.700
I can't fully express the places

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.200
to which this episode
takes our imagination,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.100
but needless to say it was
the perfect opening salvo

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.850
for the episodes to follow.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:35.220
And if it's bleak you want,

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.930
how about that one with John Hamm

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.000
that got everyone talking and then crying

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.490
and then sighing and
then basically giving up.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:43.350
What's it called?

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:44.990
The Christmas Episode?

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:45.850
Perfect.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.323
We're all in a never ending
cycle trapped inside our minds.

00:06:49.323 --> 00:06:52.223
♪ Fa la la la la la la la la ♪

00:06:52.223 --> 00:06:55.056
(Christmas music)

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.160
When one thinks of specific moments

00:06:58.160 --> 00:06:59.430
that unfolded in the show,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.370
you could look at "Striking Vipers,"

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.130
one of the comparatively happier episodes.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.410
Watching gamers fully
immersed inside a video game,

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.810
and then to see where that
action goes, it's groundbreaking.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.260
Or when we realize just how far humans

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.130
are willing to go to avoid
embarrassment, and worse,

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.720
in "Shut Up and Dance."

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.040
Webcams and video games
weren't a thing yet

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.330
when Rod Serling kicked
off "The Twilight Zone"

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.320
and his imagination didn't need them.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.580
His initial shepherding
of that show gave us

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.650
arguably as many iconic moments

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.550
as any other program in history.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.170
I'm even talking to you
Monday Night Football,

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:36.967
bum, bum, bum, bum.

00:07:36.967 --> 00:07:39.880
"Monsters Are Due on Maple
Street," "Mirror Image,"

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.460
and "Where Is Everybody"

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.230
are all landmark episodes in TV history

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.780
as is "Big Tall Wish,"

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.480
in large part due to the fact
that it was unfortunately rare

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.210
to have a black lead of a
show in the early 1960s.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.850
As fan of the program myself,

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.440
I would instantly refer
someone to William Shatner's

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.450
slow unraveling "In
Terror at 20,000 feet."

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.500
There's a man on the wing.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.090
- There's someone on the wing.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:05.120
- Or a patient waking up

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.437
to a very different looking
doctor in "Eye of the Beholder."

00:08:08.437 --> 00:08:10.820
"Twilight Zone" is at
its best in those moments

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:12.390
when the lead character

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.830
first starts to sense something is amiss.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.200
Witness Anne Francis in "The After Hours,"

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.880
realizing that she is in fact a mannequin

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.800
or even at the very end
of "Time Enough at Last,"

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.070
as Burgess Meredith has one accident

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.240
that leads to a lifetime
of mental torture.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.660
More recent incarnations
of "Twilight Zone,"

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.210
haven't born as much memorable fruit,

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:35.937
but there were a few moments.

00:08:35.937 --> 00:08:39.330
In the 1980s, guest stars like
Bruce Willis would show up

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.410
and in his episode, "Shatterday,"

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:44.410
he was directed by horror
master, Wes Craven.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.810
I'd also point you towards
the "Nightcrawlers" episode

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:48.960
and it's ultra violent conclusion

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.040
from "The Exorcist"
and "French Connection"

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:52.557
director, William Friedkin.

00:08:52.557 --> 00:08:55.380
"Twilight Zone" has always
attracted A list talent,

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.660
so when Jordan Peele
agreed to take the reins

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.980
for a new generation, it was no surprise.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.730
Already, his takes have
given us some clever updates

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:05.417
on "Twilight Zone" originals.

00:09:05.417 --> 00:09:07.080
"The Comedian," and not just

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.940
because one's talking right now,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.750
and the new and improved
"Terror at 30,000 Feet."

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:15.620
The "Replay" episode had a
darker "Groundhog Day" vibe

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.400
as does the Topher Grace
starring "Try, Try" episode.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:22.290
the best one yet in my
not humble at all opinion

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.568
would be "Blurry Man,"
starring Zazie Beetz.

00:09:24.568 --> 00:09:26.328
- You know what?

00:09:26.328 --> 00:09:27.670
I think we can beat this.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.530
- And for giggles, I do have to point out

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:33.030
that the 2002 reboot at least
had Forest Whitaker host

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.270
and there was an episode
where Katherine Heigl

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:38.160
went back in time to kill baby Hitler.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.290
I'm pretty sure even the
stuffy suits at the TEC

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.040
in the 1994 classic "Time Cop"

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.630
would have approved that mission.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:48.070
If Heigl wants to do it,
sure, send Katherine Heigl.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:49.707
I like "27 Dresses."

00:09:52.798 --> 00:09:53.631
- Mother!

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:56.300
- The shared DNA of these shows

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.450
is that beautifully twisted reveal

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:01.520
where we, the audience, are
rewarded for our suspicion

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.010
and are allowed in on the plot.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.210
In "Black Mirror's", "San Junipero,"

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.040
we're hanging on before we realize,

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.760
oh, that, ah.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.680
And in "Twilight Zone's" "Aliens,"

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.340
it's the very end when we
get conked on the head with,

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.930
wait, we're the?

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.100
These quotes weren't even
me playing coy with you

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:20.320
and not giving away spoilers.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.520
That was genuinely word
for word my reaction

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.560
when the shows gave us the Full Monty.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.603
Can I use that catchphrase
in this context?

00:10:31.967 --> 00:10:33.560
"Black Mirror" is off to a hot start,

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.650
but it's going up against over 60 years

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.010
of "Twilight Zone" memorable moments.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.860
And that's just me talking
about the Rod Serling era.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:43.537
It was like 60 years worth of greatness.

00:10:43.537 --> 00:10:45.350
"Twilight Zone" takes round two

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.160
and we're all deadlocked at one a piece.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:51.190
- No logic, no reason, no explanation,

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:52.880
just a prolonged nightmare.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.330
- Round three, cultural relevance.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:57.190
We're all tied up but "The Twilight Zone"

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:00.070
starts round three with
dynamite field position.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:02.920
If it's cultural relevance
the customer wants,

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:04.460
we don't have to go run to the back

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:05.550
and check the stock room.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.560
We have decades upon decades of greatness

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:10.190
that has influenced pretty much

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:12.940
every other show ever in some fashion.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.960
So why do I even bother
talking out round three?

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.113
Is it because I get paid by the word?

00:11:19.917 --> 00:11:22.280
"Black Mirror" does actually
have a shot in this round

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:24.140
and not just because the show name itself

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:26.710
is constantly used to
describe anything weird

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:28.230
that happens in our lives.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:29.517
Worldwide pandemic?

00:11:29.517 --> 00:11:30.740
"Black Mirror".

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:33.070
iPhone FaceTime someone all by itself?

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:34.180
That's "Black Mirror".

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.040
The McFlurry machine is always broke

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:37.610
when I roll up to Mickey D's?

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.560
That is a recurring series
of "Black Mirror" episodes,

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:41.610
and I want it to stop,

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.070
but it also resonates with the culture

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:45.410
because it shows us
where humanity, society,

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.400
and technology is going.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.150
If "The Twilight Zone" is an
alternate universe showcase

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:52.357
of basic carnal human instincts,

00:11:52.357 --> 00:11:54.770
"Black Mirror" reveals to
us where those instincts

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:56.457
are gonna be taking us.

00:11:56.457 --> 00:11:58.370
"Black Mirror" head
writer, Charlie Brooker,

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:00.290
has even pinned down current society

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:01.680
without writing an episode.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.140
He was recently quoted as saying,

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.510
he doesn't want to write
season six right now

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:07.730
because the world is too bleak,

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:10.470
but it's also a better world
for having his show in it.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:12.850
An article in "The
Independent" recently noted

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.740
that entertainment options
from "The Invisible Man"

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:18.680
to "I Am Mother to "Wounds"
and "Sorry to Bother You,"

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.270
have all been influenced by "Black Mirror"

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.340
and the show is frighteningly accurate

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:25.600
when it comes to
predicting how social media

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:27.487
can influence our world.

00:12:27.487 --> 00:12:30.030
"The National Anthem"
and "Hated in the Nation"

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.650
are both harbingers of how apps

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.540
like Twitter can be used for good or evil.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.580
And word is that China is developing

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:38.810
a networking system that is eerily similar

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.600
to one used in the "Nosedive" episode.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.180
Heck, "Waldo" is an episode

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.610
where a cartoon character
gets elected president

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.870
and fascism is allowed to rule the day.

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:49.230
- No one takes you seriously.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.410
That's why no one votes.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:51.740
- And again, "Shut Up and Dance."

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:53.550
I can do nothing in that episode,

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:56.640
but I do enjoy the Aerosmith
song of the same name.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.190
Brooker has claimed a number
of eclectic influences

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.940
on his own work with movies
like "Airplane" and "Evil Dead,"

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.720
as well as directors like Paul
Verhoeven making the list,

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:07.860
but one just needs to watch a few minutes

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:09.740
of any "Black Mirror" season to see

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:13.563
how heavily influenced it was
by the granddaddy of them all.

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:18.640
- Suspended in time
and space for a moment.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:22.010
- Zone's cultural resonance
has never been stronger,

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:24.780
stemming from creator, Rod
Serling's own experiences

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:28.420
with PTSD following his
service in World War II.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:30.850
Writing stories like the
ones found in his show

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.050
would prove to be a healing outlet

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.530
and through his work in the
realm of science fiction,

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:37.960
he was able to inject societal commentary

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:41.600
that normally wouldn't pass
the censors of his day.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.680
Racism and struggles for equality

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.710
were a hallmark of the 1960s,

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.780
but rarely made it into TV shows.

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:50.040
Serling found that by
wrapping up his stories

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.490
under the label of fantasy
and including aliens

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:53.870
and the occasional monster,

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:56.080
he could get his message
across the airwaves,

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:59.580
right under the ad agencies
unsuspecting nostrils.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:02.100
- The purpose, the point
of a dramatic show,

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.960
which is used as a vehicle
of social criticism,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:07.160
is to involve an audience to show them

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:08.770
wherein their guilt lies

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:10.550
or at least indeed their association.

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:12.830
- If you wanna just
consider the horror genre,

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.260
watch an episode like "Mirror Image"

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.260
and see the evidence
that it inspired flicks

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.627
like "Psycho" and eventually "Us."

00:14:19.627 --> 00:14:22.070
"Number 12 Looks Just
Like You" is a precursor

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:24.120
to the "Stepford Wives"
and "The Trade-Ins"

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:27.060
would help usher in the
concept of "Get Out."

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.080
The show had a huge impact on so many

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:30.600
of the artists we admire today,

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.820
from directors like Tim Burton
and M. Knight Shyamalan,

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:36.720
to writers like Stephen
King and Gene Roddenberry,

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.180
the series has been listed as

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.730
the seventh greatest show of all time

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:43.450
by "Rolling Stone" and placed fifth

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:46.580
on "TV Guide's" list of
the best dramas ever.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:50.830
And in 1997, "TV Guide" listed
"To Serve Man" at number 11

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.040
and "It's Good Life" at number 31

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.570
in their best episodes
in TV history rankings.

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:57.530
And while the legacy

00:14:57.530 --> 00:14:59.710
of "The Twilight Zone"
film is much darker,

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:01.440
as a wrongful death case was brought

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.410
on director John Landis
as a result of two actors

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:08.160
dying in an onset accident,
Landis was ultimately acquitted,

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:09.970
but that horrific day changed

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:13.070
movie set safety practices forevermore.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.060
Jordan Peele and his love for Zone

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.990
are working to continue the legacy.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:19.120
But regardless if he's successful or not,

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.160
one simply cannot overestimate

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.490
the breadth of "Twilight Zone's" impact.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:24.490
Don't believe me?

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:27.593
Go watch any "Treehouse of
Horror" Simpsons episode.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:29.383
Are you back?

00:15:29.383 --> 00:15:30.216
Okay.

00:15:30.216 --> 00:15:31.750
"Black Mirror" has a handle on technology

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:32.900
and where it's taking us,

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:34.470
but for now it's "Twilight Zone"

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.360
with the most cultural influence

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:38.040
of just about any show ever made.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.513
It wins the round and goes up two to one.

00:15:42.076 --> 00:15:43.440
- I mean, you shouldn't
say that such things

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.473
as nonsense and ridiculous.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:53.583
- Well, that's the way it goes.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:58.100
- And now it's time for the
wild card round, "The Creators."

00:15:58.100 --> 00:15:59.610
Yes, in the wild card round,

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:02.890
we're going to pit the show's
creators against each other.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:04.710
And if anyone could've seen that coming,

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.880
it's probably "The Twilight Zone"

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:09.670
or "Black Mirror" creators themselves.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:12.620
Rod Serling with his knowing
grin and smoldering cigarette

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:14.440
versus Charlie Brooker, the wunderkind,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.200
who if TV doesn't work out

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:19.300
can definitely fall back
as an Apple Store Genius.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:20.780
Rod Serling gets huge points,

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.290
not just for fighting for
our country in World War II,

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:25.550
but using his experiences and trauma

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.180
as fuel for a creative outlet

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:28.740
that would be ahead of his time.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:30.860
Years from now, we'll still marvel

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:33.480
at how much Serling got
right about humanity

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:35.500
and our most intimate biases.

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:36.587
But by then, we'll also be like,

00:16:36.587 --> 00:16:38.197
"Hey, I'm addicted to this new app

00:16:38.197 --> 00:16:40.200
"and that feels like a
"Black Mirror" episode."

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.460
In addition to being a
brilliant television writer,

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:44.760
Brooker is also an
accomplished cartoonist,

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.790
satirist and yes, even critic.

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:49.530
Is he certified on the tomato?

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.410
Maybe I'll I'll look into that.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:52.740
Get back to you.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:54.710
His comedy influences being from Britain

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.240
were naturally Monty Python,

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:58.310
and the way he can inject subtle humor

00:16:58.310 --> 00:17:01.960
into even the bleakest "Black
Mirror" episode is impressive.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:04.010
Even if the show ultimately
leaves us feeling

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.520
like our future is doomed,

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.050
the amount of variety with which

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:10.850
he can get us to that
conclusion is astounding.

00:17:10.850 --> 00:17:13.410
In episodes like "Playtest,"
it's video games.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.230
In "The History of You,"
it's a fancy contact lens.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.230
And then "USS Callister,"

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:21.270
it's just unabashed love for "Star Trek."

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.060
With Rod Serling, a hopeful ending

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.250
was a little bit less of a pipe dream,

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:26.570
but more often than not,

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:28.170
we saw the light at the end of the tunnel

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:31.687
just before a massive
curve ball blew our minds.

00:17:31.687 --> 00:17:35.327
"In to Serve Man," the aliens
are actually eating people.

00:17:35.327 --> 00:17:37.430
"Nothing in the Dark" is another classic

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:40.840
and not just because a super
young Robert Redford was in it.

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:43.590
Did Rod Serling know back
then that Bob Redford

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:46.343
would go on to be a
secret member of Hydra?

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:49.490
- Am I really so bad?

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:52.130
- The leg up that Rod
Serling will always have

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:55.050
is his vintage appearances
in each episode,

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:58.860
later, paid homage by Forest
Whitaker and Jordan Peele.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.860
Serling's handsome yet
singularly unique look

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.950
gave way to a voice
that could both comfort

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:06.310
and unsettle us in the same sentence.

00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:07.750
Whatever was about to happen

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:10.370
or no matter the nutty
event we just witnessed,

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:14.250
Serling was always there to
offer appropriate context.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.360
And when you look back
on those, it's also clear

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:18.040
he was reiterating to the audience

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:19.280
that the show we just watched

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:22.180
wasn't just about extra
terrestrials or the afterlife

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:23.870
or ventriloquist dummies.

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:25.090
It was about us.

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.280
- Any state, any entity,

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.840
any ideology that fails
to recognize the worth,

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:32.830
the dignity, the rights of
man, that state is obsolete.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.760
A case to be filed under M for mankind.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.390
- Brooker lets his tech wizardry

00:18:37.390 --> 00:18:39.250
do the talking for him most of the time,

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:40.950
but it's no less effective.

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.200
One could conceivably form a spinoff show

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.870
just based on the practical
appliance of his imagination.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:49.120
You're telling me you
wouldn't watch a YouTube show

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:52.220
about how to put your
dead partner into a robot.

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:53.210
Come on.

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:56.950
You've spent hours watching
grown adults unbox toys,

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.270
but you're telling me you
wouldn't check out soldiers

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:01.320
using the neuroimplant mass?

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:02.730
Get real y'all.

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.800
Brooker is a visionary
and I can't wait to see

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.270
where the rest of his
career takes him and us.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:10.570
But the winner of the
wild card round is the guy

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.190
who came into everyone's living room

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:15.650
via TV set once a week in the 1960s.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:17.780
America trusted Ed
Sullivan to tell the kids

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:19.530
what's going on in rock and roll

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.130
and it also trusted Rod
Serling to deliver messages

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:24.870
about ourselves wrapped
in a palatable shell,

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.410
of finely crafted horror sci-fi.

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:28.820
Rod Serling gets the point

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:30.560
and his baby goes up three to one.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:34.600
- I think it's very possible
to perform a function

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:38.550
of providing adult meaningful,
exciting, challenging drama,

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:40.750
without dealing in
controversy necessarily.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:43.400
- It was gonna be an uphill
climb for "Black Mirror" today,

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:45.260
which is not to say don't watch it.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.030
In fact, I think it's one
of the best shows on TV

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:49.790
and it's shadow looms large

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:53.050
over the current version
of "The Twilight Zone,"

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:56.430
but for all the Zones that
have ever existed in history,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:58.290
this isn't just a nostalgia win.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:19:59.830
It's an earned victory that reminds us all

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:03.940
what the 1960s were really
about, societal change,

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:07.330
through politics, laws,
music, and storytelling.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:10.830
For that, we all owe Rod
Serling a huge debt of gratitude

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:13.243
and I'm about to pay
him respect right now.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:17.930
- Submitted for your approval,

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:20.200
a voting machine, the likes
of which will determine

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.450
the winner between two
television programs.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:26.220
Although your narrator
appears to be in control,

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.590
the reality is he is
trapped in a dimension

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.590
from which he cannot escape,

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:33.343
awaiting the right comment
that may set him free,

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:36.480
or is he already free?

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.650
Fate is what we make of it,

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:43.396
both in your world and
here in the Twilight Zone.

00:20:43.396 --> 00:20:45.896
(eerie music)

